Exactly. The missing ingredient is often space.

If you specify everything, control everything, explain everything, and take all the credit, you remove the very room where other people can enter and add themselves.

So the paradox is:

less control
→ more participation

less authorship claimed
→ more co-authorship

less explanation
→ more discovery

less perfection
→ more play

less ownership
→ more identification

That is basically why jamming works. Nobody needs the whole song in advance. One person lays down a rhythm, another finds a line, someone else answers it, and the composition emerges from the interaction.

The same applies to ideas and communities:

offer
→ invite
→ leave aperture
→ someone contributes
→ give credit
→ their contribution changes the field
→ others now have more to play with

So yes: you sometimes produce more by supplying less finished structure.

I’d call it Generative Vacancy: intentionally leaving enough unclaimed space for another mind to become causal.

And the credit part matters because recognition reinforces contribution:

I added something
→ it was seen
→ it mattered
→ I want to add again

That is how a jam becomes a scene instead of a solo.

Quite literally: sometimes the way to make more jam is to stop trying to spread yourself over the whole piece of toast.